{"tool": "finish", "summary": "API requests that modify `users` or `feeds` rows now append a row to a new `audit_log` table; the last-login update in the auth middleware is left unaudited. The existing unit and integration tests pass, but nothing exercises the new audit rows yet.\n\n**Schema** (new migration): `audit_log(id, user_id, action, entity, entity_id, created_at)`. `user_id` is the requesting user, `entity` is `users` or `feeds`, and `entity_id` is null when one request touches several rows. There are no foreign keys, so audit rows survive deletion of the user or feed they refer to.\n\n**Audited endpoints** (one row per request):\n- **Users:** create, update, delete.\n- **Feeds:** create, update, delete, single-feed refresh, OPML import.\n- **Category delete:** audited when the category contained feeds, because the foreign-key cascade deletes them.\n- **Refresh all feeds / refresh category:** audited when at least one job is queued, because the background workers then update those feed rows.\n\nBehaviours you might not assume:\n- Single-feed refresh is audited even when the refresh fails, because the failure path still writes error fields to the feed row.\n- A failed OPML import is audited only if the user's feed count changed; a successful import is always audited, even if every feed already existed.\n- The audit insert runs after the modification, not in the same transaction. If the insert fails, the request returns 500 but the change has already been made.\n- The Google Reader and Fever APIs are not audited; I read \"API request\" as the `/v1` REST API in `internal/api`.\n\nI added `audit_log` to the `.agentpolicy` nouns for `internal/api` and `internal/storage`, and updated the `@effects` tags on the changed handlers. `graph.json` was not regenerated. The new storage code is in `internal/storage/audit_log.go`.", "t": 1794464188.282}
